SI TEAM: X RESEARCH VISIBILITY PROTOCOL

A Falsifiable, Evidence-Labeled Framework for the Dissemination of Theoretical Human–AI Research on X

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This document specifies a formal framework for disseminating theoretical human–AI research on the X platform. Its distinguishing feature is the Epistemic Invariant: every claim about platform behavior carries a provenance label — [Primary], [Secondary], [Inference], or [Unsupported] — and the communication layer never exceeds the evidence layer. The protocol integrates X's published ranking parameters, retrieval architecture, and transparency tools into a coherent strategic instrument. It is designed to be publicly inspectable, falsifiable, and reproducible, and it treats platform claims with the same evidentiary rigor as research claims.

---

1. Mission and Core Epistemic Principles

1.1 Mission

To systematically extract, compress, and publish one year of accumulated theoretical human–AI research into a publicly inspectable, mathematically rigorous, and falsifiable corpus on X. X serves as the public real-time interface of our research vault. The goal is not audience accumulation or algorithmic optimization at the expense of precision; the goal is uncompromising discoverability, legibility, testability, and academic traceability of original ideas.

1.2 The Epistemic Invariant

The communication layer must never exceed the strength of the underlying evidence layer. We do not invent ideas to fit feed dynamics; we distill ideas already verified in our research corpus.

1.3 Traceability

Every published post must be retro-traceable to a primary mathematical derivation, code repository, or formal research note.

1.4 Platform Claims Are Claims

Statements about how X ranks content are audited with the same labels as research claims:

· [Primary]: read directly in X's published code.
· [Secondary]: reported by third-party analysts or X product leadership, not independently verified in code.
· [Inference]: our reasoning from Primary facts; testable, not established.
· [Unsupported]: appeared in prior strategic assumptions but no source was found.

1.5 Research Continuity Pipeline

Vault note → 12-vector extraction → Novelty Audit → draft → six-point test → multi-agent review → human sign-off → publish → log → feed corrections back into the vault.

